INT. HARBORVIEW ROWING CLUB, BOATHOUSE - PRE-DAWN

Racks of eight-man shells hang from the rafters like pale fish. The river outside is black and still. Condensation beads on the windows.

COACH HAROLDINE BEAUMONT-ADEKUNLE (60s, wool cap, a stopwatch on a faded lanyard) stands at the water door with her back to the room.

Behind her, four rowers sit on the bench, lacing shoes. DARIUS KOWALA-NAKAMURA (20s, shoulders like doors) has ripped his tape twice. LORCAN FITZGIBBON-ADE (20s, hands bandaged) peels the gauze off one palm, studies the raw skin, and presses the gauze back on. ELOWEN MARCH-SANTOS (20s, her ponytail tight enough to hurt) stares at the same spot on the floor. PADDY TREMBLAY-ONYEKA (19, the youngest, a borrowed hoodie) keeps rubbing his thumbs against his knees.

Nobody speaks. The only sound is the creak of the racks and the slap of water against the dock.

Haroldine shifts her weight. She does not turn.

HAROLDINE
Boats in.

They stand. All together, with the ease of long practice, they lift the shell from its rack and walk it toward the water. Darius takes the bow. Lorcan, the stern. Elowen and Paddy each take a side. Haroldine holds the door.

The shell touches the river. Paddy steps in first. His foot slips. Elowen's hand is on his elbow before he knows he's falling.

He says nothing. She says nothing. She lets go.

HAROLDINE (CONT'D)
(to the shell, quietly)
Go.

Oars dip. The boat moves out into the fog, and the four of them breathe as one, a single lung. On the dock, the Coach clicks her stopwatch, looks at it, and clicks it again.
